BugTime AI — P1: Bug Reproduction
Extend the existing P0 application. Keep its stack and UI conventions.
Goal
From an investigation with retrieved evidence, let the developer select an existing pytest test, run it against the pinned repository commit, and inspect the results.
P1 supports existing tests. AI-generated reproduction tests come after this execution foundation works.
Features
1. Test discovery
   - Collect pytest test identifiers inside the sandbox.
   - Display test file, test name, and collection errors.
   - Prioritize tests whose files appear in retrieved evidence.
   - Let the developer select one or more tests.
2. Reproduction execution
   - Add Run reproduction to the investigation workspace.
   - Create a fresh checkout at the investigation’s pinned commit.
   - Run selected tests using structured arguments, without shell interpolation.
   - Capture stdout, stderr, exit code, duration, and pytest JUnit XML.
   - Persist results independently of the worker/container lifecycle.
3. Progress and cancellation
   - Stages: queued → preparing → collecting → running → finished.
   - Stream or poll actual execution output.
   - Support cancellation and a configurable timeout.
   - Terminate the container on cancellation or timeout.
4. Failure relevance
   - A failing test initially means “Failure observed.”
   - Let the developer mark a failure as matching the reported bug, with a short explanation.
   - Only then display “Bug reproduced — developer confirmed.”
   - Passing tests mean “Selected tests passed,” not “No bug exists.”
Execution environment
Use disposable Docker containers with:
- Non-root execution.
- Network disabled during collection and test execution.
- Default limits: 1 CPU, 1 GB RAM, 128 processes, 120-second execution timeout.
- Writable temporary checkout; read-only container root filesystem where practical.
- No application credentials or Docker socket inside the test container.
- Automatic cleanup after completion, failure, cancellation, or timeout.
Docker provides explicit resource constraints and a none network mode for these controls. Docker Docs
For the first release, use a prebuilt Python/pytest image supporting the bundled demo and dependency-compatible repositories. Show “Environment setup unsupported” for missing dependencies. Arbitrary dependency installation is a later extension.
Keep Docker access restricted to the dedicated execution worker.
UI changes
Enable the existing Tests tab.
Include:
- Discover tests button.
- Searchable test selector.
- Run and cancel actions.
- Execution stage and elapsed time.
- Passed / failed / skipped counts.
- Failed-test details and assertion traceback.
- Expandable terminal output.
- Previous attempts.
- Bug-match confirmation control and explanation field.
Keep Changes disabled until patch generation is implemented.
Data models
Add:
Model	Fields
ExecutionRun	ID, investigation ID, pinned SHA, run type, selected test identifiers, runner image digest, status, exit code, timestamps, duration, error category
TestResult	ID, execution ID, test identifier, outcome, duration, failure message, traceback
ExecutionArtifact	ID, execution ID, type, storage location, byte size, truncation flag
ReproductionConfirmation	ID, execution ID, matching failed-test identifiers, explanation, created time


Run types: collection, reproduction.
Execution statuses: queued, preparing, running, completed, failed, cancelled, timed_out.
Separate execution status from test outcome: a completed run can contain failing tests.
API additions
Under /api/v1:
Method	Endpoint	Action
POST	/investigations/{id}/test-discovery	Queue test collection
POST	/investigations/{id}/executions	Queue selected tests
GET	/investigations/{id}/executions	List attempts
GET	/executions/{id}	Status and results
GET	/executions/{id}/output	Paginated output
POST	/executions/{id}/cancel	Cancel active execution
POST	/executions/{id}/confirmation	Confirm a failure matches the bug


Validate selected identifiers against collected tests for the same pinned commit.
Result handling
Distinguish assertion failures from collection errors, interrupted execution, invalid commands, internal errors, and no tests collected. Interpret exit codes using the installed pytest version’s documented behavior. pytest documentation
Never fabricate test results. Preserve partial output when execution fails.
Acceptance criteria
P1 is complete when:
1. The demo’s seeded bug produces a real failing test with the expected assertion.
2. A known passing test produces a passing result.
3. Tests execute at the pinned SHA even after the repository branch advances.
4. Missing dependencies and collection errors are reported separately from bug failures.
5. Timeout and cancellation terminate execution and clean up containers.
6. Logs and results survive page refresh and service restart.
7. A failed test requires explicit bug-match confirmation before showing “Bug reproduced.”
8. Existing P0 flows remain functional.
9. Frontend build, lint, backend tests, and the demo reproduction flow pass.
Deliver the implementation, migrations, runner configuration, updated README, and the actual demo execution results. Stop P1 at verified reproduction; commit investigation is P2.
